{"kind":"assert","text":"CDF-2 (b5750e6e): the three answers before code.\n\n2. Yes. A member without the owner's authority can add distinct `rule` rows with no retained-byte limit below the store's own size. This answer holds the cutover deployment under b5750e6e.\n\nAn act that meets a `rule` guard reaches prepareRules (packages/scope/src/core.ts:747). A foreign scope's admitted delivery can do the same (delivery.ts:286-288). The author needs the act's grant, or the sending authority admitted by the definition's receives. The member need not hold the owner's authority.\n\nruleInput includes the fields, subjects' records and fetched facts (packages/derive/src/guards.ts:133-138). New field values yield new input digests. Each written result retains its canonical input as a `rule` row (packages/scope/src/turn.ts:438-447). Only the opt-in ruleInputBytes check bounds one such row. Nothing removes these rows. The only production forget call removes redacted texts (turn.ts:521).\n\nThe journal count still limits ordinary new acts: scopeEntries is 100,000 by default (packages/contract/src/bounds.ts:120; packages/derive/src/reserve.ts:235-248). That count is not a byte bound, and a floor can reclaim journal capacity while leaving rule rows held. The floor driver runs only with fixtureArchive (packages/scope/src/object-archive.ts:25-31). packages/scope has no per-author write rate. Even without floors, many large rule inputs can exceed the default scopeBytes of 1 GiB.\n\n1. Inputs, authors and row sizes.\n\nAll line numbers below name main a200cd0a unless marked CDF-1 head 5339ccc5. Numeric limits are the default bounds, not immutable storage limits.\n\n- `rule`, in retained_input. A genesis, act or delivery that meets a rule guard writes the input with its result. prepareRules runs at core.ts:606 and :747 and delivery.ts:286-288. A member with the needed grant, or an admitted foreign sender, can cause it. There is no constant bound on one row without the opt-in. The row includes fields, subject records and up to usesPerEntry=128 fetched entries. entryBytes is 256 KiB by default (bounds.ts:109-110). The fact portion alone can approach 32 MiB when those entries approach that size. The scope sets no separate rule-row ceiling.\n\n- `snapshot`, in retained_input. An outside operation's outcome names snapshots in its evidence. The turn retains the named snapshots (packages/scope/src/operations.ts:744-746, :808). The host supplies the answer. A member can start the workspace check through the hold capability (packages/derive/src/capability/hold.ts:625-626, :912-920). The scope checks canonical form and digest, with no byte ceiling (operations.ts:201-204). The workspace host asks for at most 4096 staged refs (packages/scope/src/workspace-host.ts:265-267). That is a count, not a scope-enforced byte bound. No code removes snapshot rows.\n\n- `value`, in retained_value. A platform act with a value place writes the supplied canonical bytes. For example, keep-configuration needs rules.publish and limits its value to CONFIGURATION_BYTES=32 KiB (packages/platform/src/rules-scope.ts:93, :245-251). Observation rows retain published extents, for example the 262144-byte domain declaration in packages/platform/src/destination.ts:472. Outside outcomes also supply values (packages/scope/src/destination-host.ts:361-362; github-host.ts:425). The scope checks each against its owner's declared max (operations.ts:738-741). The largest row is therefore domain-specific. Three shipped declarations with max 262144 are RULES_EXTENTS_VALUE_V13, RULES_TREE_PROOF and DESTINATION_CHANGED_SET (packages/platform/src/rules-policy-v13.ts:25, rules-policy-tree.ts:10, reservation.ts:54). The value-place validator requires a positive max and does not impose one global byte ceiling (packages/derive/src/validate/fields.ts:162-168). No code removes retained_value rows (packages/scope/src/sqlite.ts:90-94).\n\n- `definition`, in retained_input. Correction to builder act 67225472: ordinary acts write these rows too. valuesOf routes definition-domain values through retainedValueKey (packages/scope/src/core.ts:219-225; packages/bytes/src/retained-key.ts:28-33). Examples: activate, grant rules.activate (rules-scope.ts:264-267); open-issue and open-pr, grants issue.open and change.open, guarded by P19 (packages/platform/src/directory.ts:135, :146-158); establish, grant application.establish, with 16 dependency fields (packages/platform/src/application.ts:57-61; definition-input.ts:7-9). These shipped inputs use definitionBytes=256 KiB for each value. The earlier claim that only genesis writes at most 17 definitions is false. Nothing bounds the total number of definition rows over the scope's life.\n\n- `text`, also in retained_input, is a fifth kind the earlier lists omitted. An act or delivery with a detached text retains its canonical JSON string (packages/scope/src/core.ts:150-171, :183-187; delivery.ts:302). The text must fit its field max, with textBytes=64 KiB by default. Stored canonical JSON includes quoting and escapes, so its byte size can exceed the raw text size. A redaction alone removes the row (turn.ts:521). No retained-byte total bounds the rows today.\n\n3. Proposal: one total under scopeBytes.\n\nRename the meta row retained-entry-bytes to retained-bytes. Count canonical bytes of every newly inserted retained_input row and retained_value row, once per key. retain_value's INSERT needs RETURNING: at CDF-1 head 5339ccc5, sqlite.ts:681-693 returns 0 for values and for non-entry kinds. retainBirth already passes through retain. forget subtracts any removed row's bytes, so text redaction frees room. Keep the counter updates inside the seal transaction.\n\nKeep #retainedFull's added=0 shortcut and reserve rule (CDF-1 head turn.ts:490-493). A control delivery or non-conflict result that settles this scope's pending request passes the stop and adds its bytes, as 0c196f9a item a requires.\n\nA scope already past scopeBytes refuses only an input that adds new retained bytes and leaves the total above the bound. With CDF-1's answer shapes: an act answers refused/scope-full with name scopeBytes; a delivery answers retry/scope-full; an outcome answers unavailable/scope-full. An input adding no new retained row can still write, subject to the existing journal and opt-in checks.\n\nFor an older store, run one scan when retained-bytes is absent: sum octet_length(bytes) over retained_input and retained_value, fill retained-bytes and remove retained-entry-bytes. The constructor already performs the narrower scan at CDF-1 head sqlite.ts:177. The first turn then compares the true total.\n\nEvery retained byte write goes through retain or retainBirth, and removal goes through forget. The retained_birth association rows hold fixed-size keys, not copied canonical bytes. I propose leaving their storage overhead outside this byte total, as CDF-1 does. This proposal bounds retained canonical bytes, not physical database allocation.\n\nA settling delivery can still add a rule row without a constant size ceiling. A definition's pending-request limit bounds the number of such deliveries, but does not bound their retained rule-input size. The reserve exception therefore has no constant byte overshoot without another rule.\n\nPlanner questions before code:\na. Does an outcome settling this scope's own admitted operation pass the stop, as fits treats it (reserve.ts:243)? Proposal: yes. Today the byte stop leaves the outcome unavailable and the operation offered.\nb. Should every rule row have a byte ceiling, for example entryBytes, to bound the reserve exception's overshoot? That would add a new check and needs a ruling.\nc. Without floors, scopeEntries stops ordinary new work at 100,000 entries regardless of retained bytes. Does that limitation need a separate request?\n\nI verified these claims by reading source. I ran no tests and changed no source in this lane.\n"}